1
00:00:04,450 --> 00:00:07,998
Imagine a desert the size of the United States.

2
00:00:08,038 --> 00:00:12,234
Nine million square kilometers of sand, rock and sky.

3
00:00:13,334 --> 00:00:15,979
Now imagine standing in the middle of it.

4
00:00:16,019 --> 00:00:20,237
With a broken car, an injured friend, and twenty liters of water.

5
00:00:20,277 --> 00:00:24,953
In a place where the temperature reaches fifty degrees during the day...

6
00:00:24,993 --> 00:00:27,566
and falls close to freezing at night.

7
00:00:28,666 --> 00:00:32,379
No phone signal. No road. No footprints but your own.

8
00:00:32,419 --> 00:00:36,534
And nobody in the world who knows exactly where you are.

9
00:00:37,634 --> 00:00:39,558
This is the story of Noah.

10
00:00:39,598 --> 00:00:43,753
And of the nine days he spent stranded in the Sahara.

11
00:00:53,353 --> 00:00:58,797
After thirty-eight days lost in the Pacific Ocean, Noah made himself a promise.

12
00:00:58,837 --> 00:01:00,811
No more water. No more boats.

13
00:01:00,851 --> 00:01:05,027
If he ever went anywhere again, it would be somewhere dry.

14
00:01:06,127 --> 00:01:08,297
He should have been more specific.

15
00:01:09,397 --> 00:01:12,462
The invitation came from Doctor Yara Haddad,

16
00:01:12,502 --> 00:01:16,907
a forty-one-year-old geologist who had read about Noah's story,

17
00:01:16,947 --> 00:01:22,036
and needed a photographer for a short expedition into the deep Sahara.

18
00:01:23,136 --> 00:01:23,621
Ten days.

19
00:01:23,661 --> 00:01:27,702
Two of us, one very good car, and more water than we could ever drink.

20
00:01:27,742 --> 00:01:28,926
I study rivers, Noah.

21
00:01:28,966 --> 00:01:31,666
Rivers that disappeared thousands of years ago.

22
00:01:31,706 --> 00:01:34,480
I just need someone to take pictures of them.

23
00:01:35,580 --> 00:01:39,825
On her satellite images, Yara had found something remarkable.

24
00:01:39,865 --> 00:01:43,267
Hidden under the sand, invisible from the ground,

25
00:01:43,307 --> 00:01:45,866
were the shapes of ancient riverbeds.

26
00:01:45,906 --> 00:01:47,833
Hundreds of kilometers long.

27
00:01:47,873 --> 00:01:50,481
Branching like the veins of a leaf.

28
00:01:51,581 --> 00:01:56,552
Six thousand years ago, she explained, the Sahara wasn't a desert at all.

29
00:01:56,592 --> 00:01:57,444
It was green.

30
00:01:57,484 --> 00:02:00,739
With lakes, grasslands, and rivers full of fish.

31
00:02:00,779 --> 00:02:05,871
And she wanted to walk along one of those lost rivers with her own feet.

32
00:02:06,971 --> 00:02:10,036
They left the last town on a Tuesday morning,

33
00:02:10,076 --> 00:02:14,590
in a white four-by-four loaded with fuel, food, a satellite phone,

34
00:02:14,630 --> 00:02:16,799
and four big jerrycans of water.

35
00:02:16,839 --> 00:02:18,938
Within an hour, the road ended.

36
00:02:18,978 --> 00:02:22,785
Within three, there was nothing around them but sand.

37
00:02:23,885 --> 00:02:27,135
Noah had seen the ocean. He had seen the Arctic.

38
00:02:27,175 --> 00:02:29,946
But he had never seen anything like this.

39
00:02:29,986 --> 00:02:34,676
Dunes as tall as buildings, rolling to the horizon like frozen waves.

40
00:02:34,716 --> 00:02:39,184
And a silence so complete that he could hear his own heartbeat.

41
00:02:40,284 --> 00:02:41,135
It's beautiful.

42
00:02:41,175 --> 00:02:43,346
It's like being on another planet.

43
00:02:44,446 --> 00:02:45,336
It is beautiful.

44
00:02:45,376 --> 00:02:47,488
Just remember that it wants you dead.

45
00:02:47,528 --> 00:02:50,337
Everything out here is trying to take your water.

46
00:02:50,377 --> 00:02:53,434
The sun, the wind, the sand. Even your own breath.

47
00:02:54,534 --> 00:02:57,277
For four days, everything went perfectly.

48
00:02:57,317 --> 00:03:01,554
They camped under the most incredible stars Noah had ever seen.

49
00:03:01,594 --> 00:03:05,831
Yara found her river, exactly where the satellite had shown it.

50
00:03:05,871 --> 00:03:08,601
And Noah took hundreds of photographs.

51
00:03:13,701 --> 00:03:19,017
On the morning of the fifth day, Yara noticed something on the southern horizon.

52
00:03:19,057 --> 00:03:22,153
A thin brown line, like a smudge on the sky.

53
00:03:23,253 --> 00:03:25,249
Noah. Get in the car. Now.

54
00:03:26,349 --> 00:03:28,888
In the Sahara, they call it a haboob.

55
00:03:28,928 --> 00:03:33,209
A wall of sand and dust, sometimes more than a kilometer high,

56
00:03:33,249 --> 00:03:35,230
moving at the speed of a car.

57
00:03:35,270 --> 00:03:39,044
Pushed by a wind that can strip the paint off metal.

58
00:03:40,144 --> 00:03:41,846
Yara tried to outrun it.

59
00:03:41,886 --> 00:03:45,403
She drove north, as fast as the sand would allow,

60
00:03:45,443 --> 00:03:50,094
while the brown wall grew behind them. Taller. Darker. Closer.

61
00:03:53,694 --> 00:03:55,623
And then the world disappeared.

62
00:03:55,663 --> 00:03:58,101
Day turned into a brown, howling night.

63
00:03:58,141 --> 00:04:00,769
The sand hit the windows like a hailstorm.

64
00:04:00,809 --> 00:04:04,390
Noah couldn't see the hood of the car in front of him.

65
00:04:05,490 --> 00:04:10,590
Yara kept driving, trying to find the shelter of a rocky ridge she remembered.

66
00:04:10,630 --> 00:04:14,611
She never saw the edge of the dune. The front wheels dropped.

67
00:04:14,651 --> 00:04:17,239
The car tipped forward... and rolled.

68
00:04:18,339 --> 00:04:21,061
When it stopped, the car was on its side.

69
00:04:21,101 --> 00:04:25,427
Noah was hanging from his seatbelt. And Yara wasn't answering.

70
00:04:26,527 --> 00:04:29,192
Yara! Yara, can you hear me? Talk to me!

71
00:04:30,292 --> 00:04:33,195
My leg. Noah, my leg. I think it's broken.

72
00:04:34,295 --> 00:04:36,574
The storm raged for eleven hours.

73
00:04:36,614 --> 00:04:42,267
Noah held her hand in the dark, wrapped her leg with a jacket and two tent poles,

74
00:04:42,307 --> 00:04:45,831
and listened to the sand slowly burying the car.

75
00:04:50,931 --> 00:04:52,737
At dawn, the wind stopped.

76
00:04:52,777 --> 00:04:58,347
Noah forced the door open and climbed out into a world that had been completely

77
00:04:58,387 --> 00:04:59,318
rearranged.

78
00:05:00,418 --> 00:05:03,199
The dunes had moved. The tracks were gone.

79
00:05:03,239 --> 00:05:08,101
The car lay on its side, half buried, with sand packed inside the engine.

80
00:05:08,141 --> 00:05:13,126
And the satellite phone, which had been on the dashboard, was in pieces.

81
00:05:14,226 --> 00:05:16,976
Together, with a shovel and the car's jack,

82
00:05:17,016 --> 00:05:19,896
they managed to push it back onto its wheels.

83
00:05:19,936 --> 00:05:23,200
But the engine wouldn't start. Not even a click.

84
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:29,877
Yara's leg was badly broken below the knee. She couldn't stand. She couldn't walk.

85
00:05:29,917 --> 00:05:32,601
And she knew exactly what that meant.

86
00:05:33,701 --> 00:05:35,387
Rule number one in the desert.

87
00:05:35,427 --> 00:05:38,090
If your car breaks down, you stay with the car.

88
00:05:38,130 --> 00:05:41,253
A car can be seen from the air. A person walking can't.

89
00:05:41,293 --> 00:05:45,412
People who leave their cars are the ones they find later. Much later.

90
00:05:46,512 --> 00:05:47,489
So they stayed.

91
00:05:47,529 --> 00:05:50,525
And they counted everything they had left.

92
00:05:51,625 --> 00:05:55,150
One jerrycan of water had split open in the crash.

93
00:05:55,190 --> 00:05:57,502
Another had leaked into the sand.

94
00:05:57,542 --> 00:06:00,925
They had twenty-two liters left. For two people.

95
00:06:00,965 --> 00:06:06,058
A bag of dates, some bread, tins of sardines. A mirror. A torch. A tarp.

96
00:06:06,098 --> 00:06:07,198
A first aid kit.

97
00:06:07,238 --> 00:06:10,668
And Yara's laptop, with forty percent battery.

98
00:06:11,768 --> 00:06:13,010
Noah did the math.

99
00:06:13,050 --> 00:06:18,069
In this heat, a person can sweat more than a liter of water every hour.

100
00:06:18,109 --> 00:06:20,063
Without shade, without rest,

101
00:06:20,103 --> 00:06:24,243
twenty-two liters would last two people maybe four days.

102
00:06:25,343 --> 00:06:27,267
Somebody knows we're out here, right?

103
00:06:27,307 --> 00:06:29,156
Someone's going to come looking?

104
00:06:30,256 --> 00:06:31,848
The university knows our route.

105
00:06:31,888 --> 00:06:35,637
In five days, when we don't come back, they'll send someone. But Noah...

106
00:06:35,677 --> 00:06:38,901
the storm pushed us more than sixty kilometers off that route.

107
00:06:38,941 --> 00:06:41,091
They'll be looking in the wrong place.

108
00:06:46,191 --> 00:06:49,690
By ten in the morning, the sand was too hot to touch.

109
00:06:49,730 --> 00:06:52,895
By noon, the air above the dunes was shimmering.

110
00:06:52,935 --> 00:06:56,825
The thermometer on the dashboard read fifty-one degrees.

111
00:06:57,925 --> 00:07:02,434
The heat of the Sahara doesn't feel like heat. It feels like weight.

112
00:07:02,474 --> 00:07:06,381
It presses on your head and your chest. It dries your eyes.

113
00:07:06,421 --> 00:07:08,846
It turns every movement into work.

114
00:07:09,946 --> 00:07:13,837
They stretched the tarp from the roof of the car to the sand,

115
00:07:13,877 --> 00:07:18,671
and lay in its shadow without moving, from morning until the sun went down.

116
00:07:18,711 --> 00:07:23,440
Yara taught him the rules. Don't talk too much. Breathe through your nose.

117
00:07:23,480 --> 00:07:25,821
Ration your sweat, not your water.

118
00:07:26,921 --> 00:07:32,467
During the day, they barely moved. At night, they worked. Noah dug the wheels out.

119
00:07:32,507 --> 00:07:37,986
He laid stones on the sand in the shape of a giant arrow and the letters S, O, S,

120
00:07:38,026 --> 00:07:40,560
big enough to be seen from a plane.

121
00:07:41,660 --> 00:07:44,363
And twice a day, at sunrise and sunset,

122
00:07:44,403 --> 00:07:49,005
he climbed the highest dune and flashed the mirror at the horizon.

123
00:07:49,045 --> 00:07:51,516
In every direction. Just in case.

124
00:07:52,616 --> 00:07:54,743
On the third day, they heard it.

125
00:07:54,783 --> 00:07:57,858
A low drone, far away in the sky. An airplane.

126
00:07:57,898 --> 00:08:02,394
Noah scrambled up the dune with the mirror, flashing it at the sky,

127
00:08:02,434 --> 00:08:04,819
shouting until his voice cracked.

128
00:08:05,919 --> 00:08:08,883
It was a passenger jet. Eleven kilometers up.

129
00:08:08,923 --> 00:08:12,288
Three hundred people eating lunch, watching movies,

130
00:08:12,328 --> 00:08:17,085
looking at the desert through their windows. Not one of them saw him.

131
00:08:18,185 --> 00:08:20,116
Day three. Twelve liters left.

132
00:08:20,156 --> 00:08:22,876
Yara's leg is swollen and she has a fever.

133
00:08:22,916 --> 00:08:25,110
I'm doing everything she tells me.

134
00:08:25,150 --> 00:08:27,205
I don't know what else to do.

135
00:08:32,305 --> 00:08:34,856
On the fourth night, Yara woke him up.

136
00:08:34,896 --> 00:08:39,015
She had her laptop open, its screen glowing blue in the dark.

137
00:08:39,055 --> 00:08:41,387
The battery was at nine percent.

138
00:08:42,487 --> 00:08:44,485
Look at this. This is where we are.

139
00:08:44,525 --> 00:08:49,566
And this, this line, is the old river I came to study. It runs north. Right past us.

140
00:08:50,666 --> 00:08:55,485
On the satellite image, the ancient riverbed was a faint pale ribbon,

141
00:08:55,525 --> 00:08:57,316
snaking through the dunes.

142
00:08:57,356 --> 00:09:02,176
And forty kilometers to the north, where it met an old caravan trail,

143
00:09:02,216 --> 00:09:06,239
there was a tiny symbol from an old French map. A well.

144
00:09:07,339 --> 00:09:09,448
Wells like that are used by the nomads.

145
00:09:09,488 --> 00:09:12,147
The caravans still cross this part of the desert.

146
00:09:12,187 --> 00:09:15,202
If there's water, there are people. Sooner or later.

147
00:09:16,302 --> 00:09:20,211
You just told me rule number one is never to leave the car.

148
00:09:21,311 --> 00:09:24,432
Rule number one is for people who have time. We don't.

149
00:09:24,472 --> 00:09:27,124
At this rate, the water is gone in three days.

150
00:09:27,164 --> 00:09:29,304
And nobody is looking for us here.

151
00:09:30,404 --> 00:09:33,224
It was the hardest decision of Noah's life.

152
00:09:33,264 --> 00:09:36,349
If he stayed, they would both run out of water.

153
00:09:36,389 --> 00:09:41,403
If he went, he would leave Yara alone, injured, in the middle of the desert.

154
00:09:41,443 --> 00:09:43,598
And if he didn't find the well...

155
00:09:43,638 --> 00:09:46,315
nobody would ever find either of them.

156
00:09:47,415 --> 00:09:48,827
They split the water.

157
00:09:48,867 --> 00:09:53,528
Yara insisted he take more, because he would be walking. He refused.

158
00:09:53,568 --> 00:09:56,016
They argued in whispers for an hour.

159
00:09:56,056 --> 00:09:59,732
In the end, he took five liters. He left her seven.

160
00:10:00,832 --> 00:10:02,469
Walk at night. Rest in the day.

161
00:10:02,509 --> 00:10:06,797
Follow the old river, it will take you north. And Noah. Water remembers its way.

162
00:10:06,837 --> 00:10:08,069
Even when it's gone.

163
00:10:09,169 --> 00:10:11,114
He kissed her on the forehead,

164
00:10:11,154 --> 00:10:15,482
put five liters of water and a handful of dates into his backpack,

165
00:10:15,522 --> 00:10:17,327
and walked into the dark.

166
00:10:22,427 --> 00:10:25,993
At night, the desert is a completely different place.

167
00:10:26,033 --> 00:10:29,190
The heat drains out of the sand in a few hours.

168
00:10:29,230 --> 00:10:31,911
The temperature falls by thirty degrees.

169
00:10:31,951 --> 00:10:34,278
And above you, the sky explodes.

170
00:10:35,378 --> 00:10:38,760
Without any city lights for hundreds of kilometers,

171
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:43,591
there are so many stars that it becomes hard to find the constellations.

172
00:10:43,631 --> 00:10:45,939
The Milky Way is not a faint cloud.

173
00:10:45,979 --> 00:10:50,155
It's a river of light, bright enough to see your own shadow.

174
00:10:51,255 --> 00:10:56,612
Noah navigated the way Leon had taught him on the ocean. Find the Big Dipper.

175
00:10:56,652 --> 00:10:59,696
Follow the two stars at the end of its bowl.

176
00:10:59,736 --> 00:11:05,283
And there, alone, almost exactly above the north, is Polaris. The North Star.

177
00:11:06,383 --> 00:11:08,174
He kept it in front of him,

178
00:11:08,214 --> 00:11:13,872
slightly to the left of the pale line of the old riverbed. And he walked. For hours.

179
00:11:13,912 --> 00:11:19,556
Up the dunes, sliding down the other side, sinking to his ankles with every step.

180
00:11:20,656 --> 00:11:21,719
He wasn't alone.

181
00:11:21,759 --> 00:11:26,408
In the light of his torch, he saw tiny footprints crossing his path.

182
00:11:26,448 --> 00:11:29,925
A fennec fox, with ears almost as long as its body,

183
00:11:29,965 --> 00:11:33,770
watched him from the top of a dune and then vanished.

184
00:11:34,870 --> 00:11:40,457
And once, when he sat down to rest, something moved in the sand beside his hand.

185
00:11:40,497 --> 00:11:42,918
A yellow scorpion, its tail raised.

186
00:11:42,958 --> 00:11:45,992
Noah didn't breathe until it walked away.

187
00:11:47,092 --> 00:11:47,737
Night one.

188
00:11:47,777 --> 00:11:50,407
I think I've walked fifteen kilometers.

189
00:11:50,447 --> 00:11:52,735
It's so cold my hands are shaking.

190
00:11:52,775 --> 00:11:56,348
Two days ago I'd have given anything to feel cold.

191
00:11:57,448 --> 00:12:01,540
At dawn, he dug a shallow trench on the shady side of a dune,

192
00:12:01,580 --> 00:12:06,891
covered himself with his shirt, and tried to sleep through the heat of the day.

193
00:12:06,931 --> 00:12:10,129
He drank half a liter. Exactly half. No more.

194
00:12:15,229 --> 00:12:20,698
On the second night, the riverbed became harder to follow. The dunes grew taller.

195
00:12:20,738 --> 00:12:22,928
And Noah's body began to fail.

196
00:12:24,028 --> 00:12:28,012
Dehydration happens in stages. First, thirst and headache.

197
00:12:28,052 --> 00:12:32,452
Then dizziness, and a heart that beats too fast. Then confusion.

198
00:12:32,492 --> 00:12:34,256
You start making mistakes.

199
00:12:34,296 --> 00:12:36,337
You forget why you're walking.

200
00:12:36,377 --> 00:12:38,817
You see things that aren't there.

201
00:12:39,917 --> 00:12:43,209
On the morning of the third day, Noah saw a lake.

202
00:12:43,249 --> 00:12:47,561
A wide, shining sheet of blue water, maybe two kilometers ahead,

203
00:12:47,601 --> 00:12:51,763
with the reflections of the dunes trembling on its surface.

204
00:12:52,863 --> 00:12:54,894
He knew what it was. A mirage.

205
00:12:54,934 --> 00:12:57,793
A layer of superheated air above the sand,

206
00:12:57,833 --> 00:13:01,107
bending the light of the sky down to the ground,

207
00:13:01,147 --> 00:13:04,489
so that the sky itself looks like water. He knew.

208
00:13:04,529 --> 00:13:06,819
And he walked toward it anyway.

209
00:13:07,919 --> 00:13:10,899
The lake moved away from him as he walked.

210
00:13:10,939 --> 00:13:14,063
Always two kilometers ahead. Always shining.

211
00:13:14,103 --> 00:13:17,561
Until finally, it simply wasn't there anymore.

212
00:13:18,661 --> 00:13:23,388
That afternoon, lying in his trench, he heard Yara's voice calling him.

213
00:13:23,428 --> 00:13:29,150
Then his sister's. Then Leon's. He knew they weren't real. He answered them anyway.

214
00:13:30,250 --> 00:13:31,875
Day seven. One liter left.

215
00:13:31,915 --> 00:13:36,290
If I don't find the well tonight... I'm sorry, Yara. I'm so sorry.

216
00:13:37,390 --> 00:13:40,867
That night, he walked with the last of his strength,

217
00:13:40,907 --> 00:13:43,843
the North Star blurring in and out of focus.

218
00:13:43,883 --> 00:13:48,510
And just before dawn, he saw a shape on the horizon that didn't move.

219
00:13:48,550 --> 00:13:49,660
A ring of stones.

220
00:13:49,700 --> 00:13:53,097
And beside it, the twisted silhouette of a tree.

221
00:13:58,197 --> 00:13:59,341
It was the well.

222
00:13:59,381 --> 00:14:02,820
Exactly where the old map had said it would be.

223
00:14:02,860 --> 00:14:07,409
A circle of ancient stones, worn smooth by centuries of ropes.

224
00:14:07,449 --> 00:14:12,484
And a single acacia tree, still alive, its roots deep in the sand.

225
00:14:13,584 --> 00:14:17,933
Noah fell to his knees, leaned over the edge, and looked down.

226
00:14:19,033 --> 00:14:20,221
There was nothing.

227
00:14:20,261 --> 00:14:25,542
Just darkness, and far below, a floor of dry, cracked mud. He dropped a stone.

228
00:14:25,582 --> 00:14:28,324
He heard it hit the bottom. No splash.

229
00:14:29,424 --> 00:14:31,213
The well was dry.

230
00:14:32,313 --> 00:14:36,909
He sat with his back against the stones and drank the last of his water.

231
00:14:36,949 --> 00:14:37,939
Three mouthfuls.

232
00:14:37,979 --> 00:14:41,738
He had walked forty kilometers through the desert for this.

233
00:14:41,778 --> 00:14:43,988
For an empty hole in the ground.

234
00:14:45,088 --> 00:14:48,908
I'm sorry, Yara. It's dry. It's dry. I don't know what to do.

235
00:14:50,008 --> 00:14:54,479
And then, as the sun rose and the light crept across the stones,

236
00:14:54,519 --> 00:14:56,361
he noticed the carvings.

237
00:15:01,461 --> 00:15:05,509
On the flat rock beside the well, almost erased by time,

238
00:15:05,549 --> 00:15:07,553
someone had carved pictures.

239
00:15:07,593 --> 00:15:12,371
A giraffe, with its long neck. A herd of cattle. A man in a canoe.

240
00:15:12,411 --> 00:15:13,875
And a row of fish.

241
00:15:14,975 --> 00:15:15,319
Fish.

242
00:15:15,359 --> 00:15:17,660
In the middle of the Sahara.

243
00:15:18,760 --> 00:15:21,649
Yara had told him about carvings like these.

244
00:15:21,689 --> 00:15:24,778
They were six, seven, eight thousand years old.

245
00:15:24,818 --> 00:15:28,773
Made by people who had lived here when the Sahara was green.

246
00:15:28,813 --> 00:15:32,824
When this place was not a desert, but the bank of a river.

247
00:15:33,924 --> 00:15:38,866
And suddenly, Noah remembered what Yara had said. Water remembers its way.

248
00:15:38,906 --> 00:15:41,491
The river hadn't vanished. It had sunk.

249
00:15:41,531 --> 00:15:47,011
Deep under the sand, the old riverbed still collected what little rain fell on the

250
00:15:47,051 --> 00:15:48,829
desert. The acacia knew it.

251
00:15:48,869 --> 00:15:51,173
That was why it was still alive.

252
00:15:52,273 --> 00:15:55,584
He looked at the riverbed, at the outside of its bend,

253
00:15:55,624 --> 00:15:57,445
where water would have pooled,

254
00:15:57,485 --> 00:16:00,982
and where the acacia's roots disappeared into the ground.

255
00:16:01,022 --> 00:16:05,267
He took the small shovel from his backpack. And he started to dig.

256
00:16:06,367 --> 00:16:09,318
One hand's depth. Dry. An arm's length. Dry.

257
00:16:09,358 --> 00:16:11,290
He kept digging, in the heat,

258
00:16:11,330 --> 00:16:15,831
with his heart hammering and his vision going grey at the edges.

259
00:16:16,931 --> 00:16:22,391
And at the depth of his shoulder, the sand changed color. Darker. Cooler. Damp.

260
00:16:23,491 --> 00:16:25,521
He kept digging, then waited.

261
00:16:25,561 --> 00:16:28,876
And slowly, very slowly, a thin layer of brown,

262
00:16:28,916 --> 00:16:32,921
muddy water began to seep into the bottom of the hole.

263
00:16:34,021 --> 00:16:36,588
Water. Yara, there's water. There's water!

264
00:16:37,688 --> 00:16:40,974
He filtered it through his shirt, into his bottle.

265
00:16:41,014 --> 00:16:43,170
It tasted of mud, iron and roots.

266
00:16:43,210 --> 00:16:46,562
It was the most beautiful thing he had ever tasted.

267
00:16:46,602 --> 00:16:49,955
And then he did the second thing Yara had told him.

268
00:16:49,995 --> 00:16:54,146
He gathered dry acacia branches, and he made a fire. A big one.

269
00:16:54,186 --> 00:16:56,997
With green leaves on top, to make smoke.

270
00:17:02,097 --> 00:17:06,238
The smoke rose straight up into the windless morning sky.

271
00:17:06,278 --> 00:17:11,123
A grey column, visible for thirty kilometers in every direction.

272
00:17:12,223 --> 00:17:16,089
In the late afternoon, Noah heard bells. Faint. Rhythmic.

273
00:17:16,129 --> 00:17:21,844
And then he saw them, coming over the ridge in a long line against the golden light.

274
00:17:21,884 --> 00:17:23,968
Camels. Twenty, thirty of them.

275
00:17:24,008 --> 00:17:27,790
And men walking beside them, wrapped in indigo cloth.

276
00:17:28,890 --> 00:17:32,137
The leader of the caravan was a man named Idris.

277
00:17:32,177 --> 00:17:34,123
He was sixty-eight years old.

278
00:17:34,163 --> 00:17:37,752
He had crossed this desert more than a hundred times,

279
00:17:37,792 --> 00:17:40,629
carrying salt and dates between the oases,

280
00:17:40,669 --> 00:17:44,859
the way his father and his grandfather had done before him.

281
00:17:45,959 --> 00:17:47,819
We saw your smoke from very far.

282
00:17:47,859 --> 00:17:51,440
Only a fool or a lost man makes a fire at noon in the desert.

283
00:17:51,480 --> 00:17:53,233
You don't look like a fool.

284
00:17:54,333 --> 00:17:59,131
Idris gave him water from a goatskin bag, then made him drink slowly,

285
00:17:59,171 --> 00:18:01,865
a few sips at a time. Then he made tea.

286
00:18:01,905 --> 00:18:06,612
Because in the desert, before anything else, there is always tea.

287
00:18:07,712 --> 00:18:08,501
Three glasses.

288
00:18:08,541 --> 00:18:10,336
The first is bitter, like life.

289
00:18:10,376 --> 00:18:12,172
The second is sweet, like love.

290
00:18:12,212 --> 00:18:14,067
The third is gentle, like death.

291
00:18:14,107 --> 00:18:16,921
You drink all three, or you insult your host.

292
00:18:18,021 --> 00:18:19,413
There's a woman. Yara.

293
00:18:19,453 --> 00:18:25,007
She's with our car, forty kilometers south. Her leg is broken. She's alone. Please.

294
00:18:26,107 --> 00:18:27,144
Idris listened.

295
00:18:27,184 --> 00:18:32,168
Then he asked Noah to describe the dunes he had crossed. Their shapes.

296
00:18:32,208 --> 00:18:36,905
Their direction. The color of the sand. Noah thought he was crazy.

297
00:18:36,945 --> 00:18:38,459
Until Idris smiled.

298
00:18:39,559 --> 00:18:40,566
I know this place.

299
00:18:40,606 --> 00:18:43,647
My grandfather called it the Valley of the Old River.

300
00:18:43,687 --> 00:18:48,008
The car is between the two tall dunes that look like sisters. We leave now.

301
00:18:48,048 --> 00:18:49,710
Camels walk well at night.

302
00:18:50,810 --> 00:18:55,227
Idris sent his youngest nephew riding north on the fastest camel,

303
00:18:55,267 --> 00:18:58,245
toward the nearest village with a telephone.

304
00:18:58,285 --> 00:19:03,936
And with three men and four camels, they set off south. In the dark. Without a map.

305
00:19:03,976 --> 00:19:05,171
Without a compass.

306
00:19:05,211 --> 00:19:08,995
Guided only by the stars, and by an old man's memory.

307
00:19:14,095 --> 00:19:15,426
They rode all night.

308
00:19:15,466 --> 00:19:19,127
Noah, too weak to walk, swayed on the back of a camel,

309
00:19:19,167 --> 00:19:21,114
wrapped in an indigo blanket,

310
00:19:21,154 --> 00:19:25,348
falling asleep and waking up again under the turning stars.

311
00:19:26,448 --> 00:19:29,206
At sunrise, Idris stopped and pointed.

312
00:19:29,246 --> 00:19:32,961
Two tall dunes, side by side, exactly like sisters.

313
00:19:33,001 --> 00:19:38,378
And between them, small and white and half buried in the sand, the car.

314
00:19:39,478 --> 00:19:41,718
Noah slid off the camel and ran.

315
00:19:41,758 --> 00:19:45,353
He fell, got up, fell again. The tarp was still up.

316
00:19:45,393 --> 00:19:50,460
The stones of the SOS arrow were still there. But nothing was moving.

317
00:19:51,560 --> 00:19:52,860
Yara! Yara!

318
00:19:53,960 --> 00:19:59,562
She was lying in the shade of the tarp, her eyes closed, her lips cracked and grey.

319
00:19:59,602 --> 00:20:04,511
Beside her were two water bottles. One empty. And one completely full.

320
00:20:05,611 --> 00:20:07,935
Noah? You came back. I saved some. For you.

321
00:20:07,975 --> 00:20:09,775
In case you came back thirsty.

322
00:20:10,875 --> 00:20:15,764
She had been rationing her water to a few sips a day. Not to survive longer.

323
00:20:15,804 --> 00:20:21,207
But so that, if Noah came back with nothing, there would still be enough for him.

324
00:20:22,307 --> 00:20:27,648
That afternoon, two military trucks and a medical team arrived from the north,

325
00:20:27,688 --> 00:20:29,373
guided by Idris's nephew.

326
00:20:29,413 --> 00:20:32,132
Yara was flown to a hospital that night.

327
00:20:32,172 --> 00:20:37,772
The doctors said that one more day, maybe two, and she would not have survived.

328
00:20:38,872 --> 00:20:41,329
Noah lost nine kilos in nine days.

329
00:20:41,369 --> 00:20:44,120
Yara needed two operations on her leg.

330
00:20:44,160 --> 00:20:47,425
And a year later, she published her research.

331
00:20:47,465 --> 00:20:53,638
A map of an ancient river, three hundred kilometers long, buried under the Sahara.

332
00:20:54,738 --> 00:21:00,579
She named the well where Noah found water after the man who had saved them both.

333
00:21:00,619 --> 00:21:05,431
Today, on scientific maps of the Sahara, there is a tiny blue dot.

334
00:21:05,471 --> 00:21:09,444
And beside it, a name. Bir Idris. The well of Idris.

335
00:21:10,544 --> 00:21:14,599
Every winter, Noah gets a photograph from Yara's phone.

336
00:21:14,639 --> 00:21:18,173
A caravan of camels, crossing a ridge at sunset.

337
00:21:18,213 --> 00:21:22,011
And three glasses of tea, on a tray, in the sand.

338
00:21:23,111 --> 00:21:28,078
Because sometimes, the things that save us are older than we can imagine.

339
00:21:28,118 --> 00:21:33,154
A river that disappeared six thousand years ago. A map drawn by the stars.

340
00:21:33,194 --> 00:21:36,293
And a stranger who still remembers the way.

341
00:21:42,393 --> 00:21:45,812
If you were stranded in the desert with a broken car...

342
00:21:45,852 --> 00:21:48,013
would you stay and wait for rescue?

343
00:21:48,053 --> 00:21:50,026
Or would you walk into the dark,

344
00:21:50,066 --> 00:21:55,436
following a river that disappeared thousands of years ago? Tell us in the comments.

345
00:21:56,536 --> 00:21:57,580
And remember...

346
00:21:57,620 --> 00:22:00,587
when everything ends, the story begins.
